  "prompt_text": "The figure is a flow-style comparison chart linking ambient energy sources to harvesting methods, storage options, and possible low-power uses. It starts with inputs such as solar, wind, vibration, raindrop, and radio-frequency energy, each shown with rough power-density ranges. These feed into transduction methods including photovoltaic, electromagnetic, piezoelectric, triboelectric, and RF antenna approaches, which are annotated with approximate conversion efficiencies.\n\nThe harvested energy is then matched to storage technologies—battery, supercapacitor, and capacitor—highlighting their energy density, efficiency, and voltage ranges. Finally, the chart connects these storage choices to outputs in three broad categories: propulsion, computing, and sensing. Example loads include motors and actuators for propulsion, microcontrollers and communication modules for computing, and sensors such as radar, cameras, ultrasonic/IMU/GPS units, and barometric sensors.\n\nOverall, the graphic emphasizes how different environmental sources vary greatly in available power, how harvesting methods differ in efficiency, and how storage technology helps determine what kinds of functions can realistically be powered. It visually suggests that stronger sources like solar support a wider range of applications, while weaker sources are better suited to intermittent, low-power electronics and sensing.",
